% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the `@dataclass` constructor
% synthesiser in `pdd/conformance/dataclass_signatures.py`.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Responsibility
  Producing the parameter list of the `__init__` that `@dataclass` would generate
  for a class that does not define one, from the AST alone. Without it, renaming a
  dataclass field would change the constructor ABI with nothing to diff.

% Non-responsibilities
  Does not decide what is public and does not format non-dataclass signatures.

% Vocabulary
  - **synth**: the reconstructed constructor parameter list.
  - **own field**: an annotated assignment in the class's own body.

% Contract rules
  R1. An explicit `__init__` in the class body MUST win; synthesis is only for
      classes lacking one.
  R2. Own fields MUST appear in source order - the order the stdlib uses.
  R3. `@dataclass(kw_only=True)` MUST make every field keyword-only. The bare and
      `dataclasses.`-qualified decorator spellings, called or not, MUST all be
      recognised.
  R4. An in-body `KW_ONLY` sentinel field MUST split the list: fields before it
      stay positional, fields after become keyword-only. It MUST be recognised
      before any underscore-prefix exclusion, because the sentinel is
      conventionally named `_`. Both the bare and `dataclasses.`-qualified
      annotation spellings count.
  R5. When both the decorator flag and the sentinel are present, the result MUST
      be keyword-only exactly once - the decorator wins. A keyword-only marker
      with no fields after it MUST NOT appear in the output.
  R6. `InitVar` fields MUST be included: they are real constructor parameters even
      though not stored as attributes. `ClassVar` fields MUST be excluded per
      PEP 557. A field defaulted with `field(init=False, ...)` MUST be excluded,
      since the generated constructor omits it; `init=True`, an absent `init`, or
      any non-`field` default keeps the field.
  R7. Each parameter MUST carry its annotation and default as written, so a
      change to either diffs. An `InitVar[int]` to `int` flip therefore shows up
      naturally.
  R8. Inherited fields MUST be merged in REVERSE-MRO order, matching how
      `@dataclass` populates `__dataclass_fields__` by walking bases
      right-to-left. For `class C(A, B)` with one field each, the synth is
      `(b, a, c)`.
  R9. A base decorated `@dataclass(init=False)` MUST STILL contribute its fields
      to a derived class. `init=False` suppresses only that base's own
      constructor synthesis, while its fields remain in `__dataclass_fields__`.
      A non-dataclass base contributes nothing.
  R10. A base that cannot be resolved to a class in the same module - a
       cross-module import, or an attribute-form reference like `pkg.Base` - MUST
       be marked as unresolved in the synth, so local field changes still diff
       while no false authority is claimed over the imported base's fields. A
       free name that is neither a same-module class nor imported, such as the
       `object` builtin, contributes nothing.
  R11. When a derived class redeclares a base's field, the derived annotation and
       default MUST win but the parameter MUST keep the base's original position -
       dict insertion-order semantics.
  R12. Self-referential bases MUST NOT cause unbounded recursion. `class A(A)` is
       illegal at runtime but representable in the AST.

% Dependencies
  Standard library `ast` only. Called by `signatures.py`; MUST NOT import it.

% Accepted limitation
  `KW_ONLY` sentinel propagation across an inheritance boundary is not modelled -
  a derived class re-emits its own marker.
